Q: Callbacks in no_std, no_alloc Rust A: <p>I've ended up using <code>core::sync::atomic::AtomicPtr</code> to store function pointers.</p>
<pre><code>static CHANNEL_IRQ_HANDLERS: [AtomicPtr&lt;fn (usize)&gt;; CHANNEL_COUNT] = [const { AtomicPtr::new(irq_handler_noop as *mut fn(usize)) }; CHANNEL_COUNT];

fn irq_handler_noop(_ch: usize) {}

fn set_channel_irq_handler(handler: Option&lt;fn (usize)&gt;, ch: &amp;Peri&lt;impl Channel&gt;) {
    let handler = if let Some(handler) = handler {
        handler
    } else {
        irq_handler_noop
    };
    CHANNEL_IRQ_HANDLERS[ch.number() as usize].store(handler as *mut fn(usize), Ordering::Relaxed);
}
</code></pre>
<p>And calling per channel handlers in irq looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>let channel_handler = CHANNEL_IRQ_HANDLERS[channel].load(Ordering::Relaxed);
let channel_handler: fn (usize) = unsafe { core::mem::transmute(channel_handler) };
channel_handler(channel);
</code></pre>
<p>I'm not 100% sure that ordering I've used is correct but the code compiles and works fine. I'm not going to check that as in the meantime the IRQ handling was reworked to work differently. Anyway, this concludes this question.</p>
<p>Thanks to all of you who commented/answered. Your answers helped me to learn and write this solution.</p>

Q: How to create a copy of table in PostgreSQL? A: <p>You are probably looking for <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/static/sql-createtableas.html" rel="noreferrer">CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</a>, e.g.:</p>

<pre><code>CREATE TABLE copy_table AS SELECT * FROM original_table;
</code></pre>
